\p{The diagram includes the planned
[Gaussian laws at every prime](fdip-008M). The arithmetic families below
already supply a [fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) to the
[realization comparison](fdip-004L); the prime-by-prime extension
still poses questions.}

\p{Which constructions survive if field values use finite arithmetic
instead of the real numbers? For each prime #{p}, the finite field
#{\mathbb F_p} gives such a value system. Comparing it with a real model
requires a specified arithmetic family and control of denominators.
The four-cycle coefficients give an [arithmetic family](fdip-000U)
over #{\mathbb Z[1/2,t]}. Scalar extension organizes its
[Clifford algebras](fdip-000V), while the form, elimination map and
observable ratio each impose their own domain.
[Bad fibers](fdip-000W) distinguish those domains concretely.
This base excludes characteristic two, where the earlier
[half-turn example](fdip-000N) shows how an algebra can survive
while its sector decomposition changes.}

\p{The rational comparison uses [Gaussian elimination](fdip-0018)
and the [reference pairing](fdip-001N) and [trace conventions](fdip-0019).
It distinguishes the information in a [quadratic center character](fdip-0049)
from the independently defined trace observable.}

\p{With rational positive-definite precision in counting coordinates,
the Gaussian covariance #{Q^{-1}} is rational, although its normalized
real law still uses real integration. Which moment and partition-function
identities have arithmetic counterparts? The planned
[prime-by-prime Gaussian chapter](fdip-008M) asks this next question;
its answers are not yet supplied.}

